_MANGO_PASSAGE = (
    "Set:1 A farmer had a rectangular land containing 205 trees. He distributed that land "
    "among his four daughters - Abha, Bina, Chitra, and Dipti - by dividing the land into "
    "twelve plots along three rows (X, Y, Z) and four columns (1, 2, 3, 4) as shown in the "
    "figure below. The plots in rows X, Y, Z contained mango, teak, and pine trees, "
    "respectively. Each plot had trees in non-zero multiples of 3 or 4, and none of the "
    "plots had the same number of trees. Each daughter got an even number of plots. In the "
    "figure, the number mentioned in the top left corner of a plot is the number of trees in "
    "that plot, while the letter in the bottom right corner of the plot is the first letter "
    "of the name of the daughter who got that plot. For example, Abha got the plot in row Y "
    "and column 1 containing 21 trees. Some information in the figure got erased, but the "
    "following is known:\n\n"
    "Grid (row X = mango, row Y = teak, row Z = pine; blank = erased):\n"
    "| | Col 1 | Col 2 | Col 3 | Col 4 |\n"
    "|---|---|---|---|---|\n"
    "| X | 12 trees, Chitra | - | - | - |\n"
    "| Y | 21 trees, Abha | - | - | -, Abha |\n"
    "| Z | -, Bina | -, Chitra | 9 trees | 28 trees |\n\n"
    "1. Abha got 20 trees more than Chitra but 6 trees less than Dipti.\n"
    "2. The largest number of trees in a plot was 32, but it was not with Abha.\n"
    "3. The number of teak trees in Column 3 was double that in Column 2 but was half of "
    "that in Column 4.\n"
    "4. Both Abha and Bina got a higher number of plots than Dipti.\n"
    "5. Only Bina, Chitra, and Dipti got corner plots.\n"
    "6. Dipti got two adjoining plots in the same row.\n"
    "7. Bina was the only one who got a plot in each row and each column.\n"
    "8. Chitra and Dipti did not get plots which were adjacent to each other (either in row, "
    "column, or diagonal).\n"
    "9. The number of mango trees was double the number of teak trees."
)

